### Community Note

* Please vote on this issue by adding a 👍 [reaction](https://blog.github.com/2016-03-10-add-reactions-to-pull-requests-issues-and-comments/) to the original issue to help the community and maintainers prioritize this request
* Please do not leave "+1" or "me too" comments, they generate extra noise for issue followers and do not help prioritize the request
* If you are interested in working on this issue or have submitted a pull request, please leave a comment

<!--- Thank you for keeping this note for the community --->

### Description

The `azurerm_key_vault_certificate` resource allows for ECDSA certificates with a key type of `EC`, but unlike `azurerm_key_vault_key` it doesn't let the user specify which elliptic curve to use.

The [v7.1 Create Certificate API](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/keyvault/createcertificate/createcertificate) adds a `crv` attribute to the [KeyProperties](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/keyvault/createcertificate/createcertificate#keyproperties) type, so I've raised a [draft PR](https://github.com/terraform-providers/terraform-provider-azurerm/pull/10867) that updates the `keyvault` service to the v7.1 API and adds `curve` to the allowed key properties, matching the behaviour of `azurerm_key_vault_key` wherever possible.

All the `keyvault` acceptance tests pass (including new coverage for `curve`), but I'm raising this issue to get feedback on some implications of the change:

1. The API update introduces a breaking change for users of `azurerm_key_vault_key` by replacing `keyvault.SECP256K1` with `keyvault.P256K`. The CLI accepts both `"SECP256K1"` and `"P-256K"` as key types, but the SDK doesn't. I can think of ways to hack around this but that seems worse in the long term than accepting the upstream change (which also makes us consistent with the portal).
2. For consistency with `azurerm_key_vault_key` I've added the same validation to `key_type`, which in particular means that `RSA` now has to be uppercase. The current code accepts `rsa` and creates a working RSA certificate, so the validation change potentially breaks working configurations - is that acceptable? The alternative is to remain inconsistent and deal with this when the platform stops accepting lowercase for certificates.
3. As written the PR requires the user to specify a matching `key_size` for the curve type, which is redundant - for example, [CLI policies](https://gist.github.com/simonbrady/b30522b25aa8c818d6a6b73e50fbe8d0) don't require this. [This diff](https://gist.github.com/simonbrady/82c0805acc8a32665fcc6e4bfea54d9c) on top of the PR attempts to infer `key_size` from the value of `curve`, but it doesn't quite do the job because `key_size` is still `null` in the parsed configuration so we get plan instability (see comment at lines 37-42). Can anyone suggest an example I can follow to achieve this?

### New or Affected Resource(s)

Directly affected:

* `azurerm_key_vault_certificate` resource and datasource

Indirectly affected by API update:

* All other `azurerm_key_vault_*` resources and datasources

